This document defines coverage. Hours remain separate so that a covered specialty is not confused with continuous availability.

## Reading coverage by plan

The {{ seed.plans.consumer.essential.name }} plan focuses its clinical coverage on {{ seed.specialties.general_practice.name | lower }}. An active account alone does not support an affirmative answer about another specialty.

The {{ seed.plans.consumer.family.name }} plan adds {{ seed.specialties.pediatrics.name | lower }} and {{ seed.specialties.dermatology.name | lower }} to {{ seed.specialties.general_practice.name | lower }}. Dependent limits and family registration remain in another document even though the plan name suggests family use.

The {{ seed.plans.consumer.premium.name }} plan contains all seven registered specialties. For {{ seed.specialties.psychology.name | lower }} and {{ seed.specialties.nutrition.name | lower }}, the knowledge base also defines a monthly included quantity. That quantity is a plan benefit, not a promise that every time slot is available.

## Employer plans

Employer tiers must first map to their consumer plan. The resulting plan supplies the specialty rules. The assistant must not associate an employer tier with a specialty without retrieving the approved mapping.

## Decision examples

This page can confirm that a {{ seed.plans.consumer.family.name }} user has dermatology coverage. Answering whether dermatology operates on Sunday also requires the hours document.

This page confirms both psychology coverage and the included monthly quantity for a {{ seed.plans.consumer.premium.name }} user. The schedule evidence remains necessary to say when a consultation can occur.

If a question combines plan coverage, a monthly allowance, and a requested day, each part needs its own supporting passage. The plan name alone cannot prove the quantity or the schedule. A complete response may therefore cite this document more than once and add the hours document without treating either source as a substitute for the other.

## Claim limits

Coverage does not guarantee a prescription, immediate care, or a clinical decision. It does not authorize the assistant to invent specialties beyond the seven listed here. If a requested service is absent, this knowledge base provides no evidence that the plan includes it.
